{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Downtrend/mean-reversion miss: when the recent 30d history shows a peak followed by weakening (lower highs or a sharp drop), the model stays too high and too flat. Apply a downward bias that increases with horizon (small in days 1–7, larger in days 8–30).",
    "Overshoot after spikes: after a short-term rally/spike in history, the model extrapolates elevated levels (often +5 to +10 EUR by weeks 3–4). Damp the forecast by scaling deviations from the last price toward the 30d mean (e.g., shrink forecast changes by ~30–50% beyond day 10).",
    "Turning-point lag: the model reacts late to regime shifts (it keeps forecasting continuation while reality reverses). If last 5–10 days show momentum reversal (e.g., price up but returns decelerating / or price down but selling pressure accelerating), flip the sign of the model’s incremental trend for the next 1–2 weeks.",
    "Horizon-specific drift: errors are modest in the first week but become strongly positive bias in weeks 2–4 (model > actual). Introduce a horizon ramp correction: subtract an increasing offset with lead time (e.g., ~0–1 EUR days 1–7, ~2–4 EUR days 8–14, ~5–8 EUR days 15–30, scaled by recent volatility).",
    "Volatility underestimation on sell-offs: true outcomes show sharper declines than forecast during drawdowns. When recent realized volatility is high and downside moves dominate, widen downside moves in the forecast (increase negative daily steps; don’t cap declines)."
  ]
}
